# Abstract

Critical phenomena represent one of the deepest mysteries in quantum field theory and statistical physics. Near critical points, microscopic details become irrelevant, correlation lengths diverge, fluctuations become scale invariant, and systems belonging to entirely different physical domains exhibit identical behavior.

Within conventional approaches, critical points are characterized by nonanalyticities of thermodynamic potentials and classified by universality classes derived from renormalization group fixed points.

Unified Finite-Density Quantum Field Theory (UFD-QFT) proposes a deeper interpretation. Critical endpoints are geometric singularities of density space. They occur when the thermodynamic manifold undergoes a bifurcation in topology, renormalization flow, statistical gauge structure, and thimble organization simultaneously.

This document develops a geometric theory of critical phenomena in which universality emerges from singular geometric structures rather than microscopic dynamics. Critical endpoints become density-space singularities where multiple geometric sectors merge, separate, or reorganize.

